
Each exercise contains a program (described below) requiring you to insert OpenMP directives into the
code and then noting how performance changes as the thread count changes. These are not complex examples. They
are just meant to get you used to using the OpenMP syntax.

Before you begin, edit the ENV file to choose whether or not you wish to do with exercises with C or with Fortran.
All you need to do is uncomment the compiler option that you wish to use, e.g to use the C version of each exercise
your ENV file will look like this:

#!/bin/bash
export COMPILER=cc
#export COMPILER=ftn

The ENV file exists to make the use of our build and run scripts easy and simple.

Building, compiling and running each example is trivial. Make your changes and then type

./runit N

where N is the number of threads that you wish to use. The script will compile your code, create a batch script,
and launch the job. 

Exercises:
===========
===========

Ex1:
=====

This routine calculates the value of pi by integrating a function in a do/for loop. 
Your tasks are to
a) Insert directives to parallelize the do/for loop.
b) Insert directives to use a reduction on the loop in order to capture the value of the integral.
c) Pay attention to any private variables needed.
d) Run the code for a few different thread counts (e.g. 1,2,4,8,etc).

Ex2:
=====

Thix exercise introduces some more worksharing constructs
The Fortran and C versions of this exercise are slightly different:

Fortran:
Your tasks are to
a) Parallelize the initializations using the OpenMP workshare directive
b) Parallelize the function calls using the sections directive
d) Run the code for a few different thread counts (e.g. 1,2,4,8,etc).

C:
Your tasks are to
a) Parallelize the initializations using the OpenMP sections directive (or, just parallelize them)
b) Parallelize the function calls using the sections directive
d) Run the code for a few different thread counts (e.g. 1,2,4,8,etc).

Ex3:
=====

This exercise requires you to parallelize a matrix-matrix multiply. It also introduces the use
of a couple of the most commonly used run time library functions: omp_get_thread_num and omp_get_num_threads.
Your tasks are to:
a) Parallelize a region, making sure each thread gets its own thread number. Examine the code first to look over which
the scope of the thread number should be valid.
b) parallelize the initializations (using sections or parallel for loops).
c) For the first print statement, just use a single thread to do the print.
d) Parallelize the matrix-matrix multiply using a do/for directive.
d) Run the code for a few different thread counts (e.g. 1,2,4,8,etc).


Ex4:
=====

This exercise requires you to parallelize two different nested loops containing arrays with different dimensions
but nearly the same total amount of work. 
Your tasks are to:
a) Parallelize each nested loop
b) Run the code for a few different thread counts (e.g. 1,2,4,8,etc).
c) Note the difference in scaling between the loops.
d) Insert a collapse directive with a depth of 2 (i.e. collapse(2)) in each OpenMP do/for construct.
e) Run the code for a few different thread counts (e.g. 1,2,4,8,etc).
f) Note the difference in scaling between the loops. You should see hardly any change in the first loop and a big improvement
in the second loop.


Ex5:
=====

Before you start this exercise, do this:

module swap PrgEnv-pgi PrgEnv-cray

if you are using the PGI compiler. We just need to do this since PGI doesn't support tasking at the moment.

This exercise introduces the concept of tasking. The code itself calculates the value of the Fibonacci function for n=41.
This version of the algorithm uses recursive calls, making parallelization a bit trickier. This isn't necessarily the way
that you'd do this in reality....
Your tasks are to:
a) Parallize the initial call to fib() in the main routine. Think about how many threads you want calling fib() initially.
Just because you create a team of threads, it doesn't mean you need to use them all and, with tasks, the others won't sitting
around doing nothing if they are required to do work later.
b) Add some tasks directives to the fib() function so that each call to fib() is it's own task.
c) Think about the attributes of n1 and n2. Shared, or private?
d) Do you need a barrier? What kind of barrier?



